{"type": "thinking", "text": "Now:\n- 1/2 record: \"Oral · 1/2 tablets · 1 mg\" (1/2 × 2 mg = 1 mg) correct.\n- 1/3 record: \"Oral · 1/3 tablets · 0.67 mg\" (1/3 × 2 = 0.6666 → 0.67). Hmm, \"0.67 mg\". Is that expected? Rounding to 2 decimals. Fine maybe.\n\nBut the fraction formatting \"1/3 tablets\" and \"1/2 tablets\" — plural. Is that intended? The \"single general rule\" might be about showing fractions as \"1/2\" in both editor and cards. The plural \"tablets\" perhaps because the unit label is always \"tablets\". Hard to judge.\n\nWait — the card says \"0.67 mg\" for 1/3 tablet of 2 mg. Is that the right dose? 0.666... rounds to 0.67. Fine.\n\nPotential bug: \"1/2 tablets\" vs \"0.5 tablets\" - grammar. But maybe the general rule intended \"½ tablet\". I'm not sure. The changelog says \"pill fractions are now formatted through a single general rule.\" Maybe previously different places formatted fractions differently; now a single rule. We can check consistency between editor and cards: editor shows \"1/3\", card \"1/3\" — consistent. Between medicine list \"Tablet · 2 mg\" no fraction. Hmm.\n\nLet me test 1/8 and 1/4. Also see if there's a weird case: fraction like 0.375? Not available. The seekbar seems discrete 1/8,1/4,1/3,1/2,1. \"1/8 tablets\" would be plural too.\n\nBut maybe the actual bug in the fraction rule: for 1/2, it shows \"1/2 tablets\" instead of \"½ tablet\"? Hmm. I shouldn't file speculative style issues unless clearly wrong. Let's look closer at the dosing subtitle: \"Oral · 1/2 tablets · 1 mg\". If you take half a tablet, \"1/2 tablets\" is grammatically wrong; a \"single general rule\" would typically handle singular/plural. Could be accepted as a UI bug. But let me search for more deterministic issues.\n\nLet me test fractions 1/8, 1/4 and see. Add another record with 1/8? That would create more records; allowed as testing. But adding many records clutters. We can delete them after (Delete in edit record). Let's set 1/8 on the new record by editing it? The Edit record sheet only allows date/time, not fraction. Hmm. So to get 1/8 we'd need to add another log record with fraction 1/8. Let's do a couple and delete later.\n\nActually, maybe more valuable: test the \"single general rule\" for values where fraction and unit combine: \"1/8 tablets\". Let's add one with 1/8.\n\nGo to Home pencil again."}
