{"type": "thinking", "text": "The Home screen DOES scroll! My previous swipes started too low (on cards / near bottom nav) and were intercepted. Now it scrolled and all 5 records are visible, including the injection card. So the Home cut-off bug is NOT real — the list scrolls; my gestures were bad. Good thing the dev couldn't reproduce. I should not have filed that report. It was rejected, fine.\n\nHowever, there is one issue: the scroll from y=1200 moved the chart off and the 3rd card etc. Now all records reachable. So no bug. Great.\n\nNow note: the initial screen shows a focused \"Chart overview\" at (485,421) after scroll; fine.\n\nLet me reset scroll to top (swipe down on chart area). Then continue.\n\nAreas to test remaining:\n- Medication cards/list consistency: card subtitles vs record details vs medicine list. The \"1 tablets\" bug logged.\n- Home scroll works.\n- Rotation: state kept (yes).\n- Medicines list header with all categories: verified.\n- Category picker: verified across all.\n- Prep form picker: found primary bug (unresolved default). Also found Ampule label \"Vial strength\" but dev can't reproduce. Hmm.\n- Maybe there's a bug with the \"Preparation type\" explicit pick and persistence: after selecting Vial and saving, the medicine list shows \"Injection · Vial · ...\" correct. For Ampule, \"Injection · Ampule · 5 mg\" correct.\n- Check gel/patch saving. Let me create a Gel (Sachet) medicine and a Patch medicine to verify list subtitles and card. Could reveal formatting issues like \"Gel · Sachet · 0.1% · 1.0g\" or \"Patch · Release rate · 100 μg/day\". The \"μg\" unit is unusual. Let's do it.\n\nBut first, the primary unresolved-subtype bug: We reported it, dev confirmed and logged. Good.\n\nNow think about the \"pill fractions\" rule more. We found \"1 tablets\". Are there other cases? Let's see how the medicine list displays a medicine with a fractional default dose? Medicine list shows \"Tablet · 2 mg\" only. Card subtitle uses \"1/2 tablets\". The singular/plural issue applies to 1 and fractions. The report logged \"1 tablets\". Good.\n\nWhat about the \"Count\" field and \"Tablets per dose\" both present in the Add record editor: a user might think Count is tablets per dose. Not in changed areas.\n\nWhat about the \"single general rule\" for fractions in the editor vs card: Editor shows \"1/2\", card shows \"1/2\". Consistent. But what about the decimal dose: 0.67 mg, 0.25 mg — are these correctly computed? 1/3 × 2 = 0.666 → 0.67 (round). 1/8 × 2 = 0.25. Good.\n\nWhat about the \"1/8\" value: Editor displays \"1/8\". Card displays \"1/8\". Good. If the general rule were meant to reduce fractions like 2/8 → 1/4, all provided values are reduced. Fine.\n\nMaybe test the fraction with strength 1 mg: 1/3 × 1 = 0.33 mg. Fine.\n\nPotential bug: For 1/2 tablet of 2 mg, dose 1 mg, but card said \"1 mg\". correct.\n\nWhat about \"0.67 mg\" rounding: With 1/3 × 2 = 0.6667 → displayed 0.67. Fine.\n\nNow maybe test the \"Count\" >1: set Count 2 for a record and see card subtitle. Does the card subtitle multiply tablets or show \"2\"? The Count may be number of doses; perhaps the card should show \"1/2 tablets · 2\" or \"Count 2\". Let's test by adding a record with per-dose 1/2 and Count 2, see subtitle and mg. This is within medication cards/dosing subtitle. Let's do.\n\nAlso test the increment on Count: Use Home pencil → EV 2mg → set tablets per dose 1/2 and count 2 → save. Then see Plan card. Hmm, we already have records; adding another is fine.\n\nLet me first scroll home back to top and then use the pencil. Actually current Home scrolled; the pencil is at top app bar still visible (element 24). Use it."}
